Pulmonary Hypertension
Respiratory System
Exertional syncope, Loud P2, Right ventricular heave, Elevated mPAP

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-040
Reason for Visit: Frightening episode of near-fainting during mild exertion accompanied by severe, worsening leg swelling and extreme fatigue, requiring urgent cardiovascular evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a history of increasing breathlessness who today experienced exertional presyncope and rapid onset of massive peripheral edema. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute decompensated right heart failure and severe pulmonary hypertension before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I get so winded just walking up the driveway that my vision goes black and I feel like I'm going to pass out, and my legs are swelling up rapidly.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing steadily over the past 6 months. The patient reports severe, progressive dyspnea on exertion. They complain of profound exertional fatigue and weakness that limits their daily activities. Over the last week, they noted rapidly increasing bilateral pitting peripheral edema. Today, the patient experienced a terrifying episode of exertional presyncope (near-fainting) while attempting to walk up a slight incline. They do not report any chronic cough, wheeze, or purulent sputum.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; currently afraid to ambulate due to dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of syncope resulting in traumatic falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 20 breaths/min | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 88 Kg (Increased 4kg in 10 days)
General: Appears chronically fatigued, breathing comfortably at rest but noticeably distressed when moving to the examination couch. Mild cyanosis of the lips is present.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular examination reveals a prominent right ventricular heave (parasternal lift). Auscultation demonstrates a loud pulmonary component of the second heart sound (loud P2) and a pansystolic murmur consistent with tricuspid regurgitation. There is an elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP) with prominent waves. Respiratory auscultation is completely clear bilaterally. Lower extremities exhibit severe 3+ bilateral pitting edema to the thighs.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of reduced mobility; mobility aids placed near the patient's chair.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter vitamins; previously ceased a prescribed diuretic due to inconvenient frequent urination.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pulmonary Hypertension - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Left-Sided Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely clear lung fields (absence of pulmonary edema/crackles) despite the massive systemic fluid overload and elevated JVP.

Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung fields without wheeze, rhonchi, or hyperinflation, and the primary symptom is syncope rather than a productive cough.

Aortic Stenosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of an ejection systolic murmur radiating to the carotids, and the presence of overwhelming right-sided heart failure signs (loud P2, heave).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, ominous signs of advanced Pulmonary Hypertension leading to acute right heart failure (cor pulmonale). The clinical tetrad of exertional syncope, a loud P2, a right ventricular heave, and profound right-sided fluid overload is clearly evident. The exertional presyncope indicates a fixed, critically low cardiac output from the right ventricle, meaning the heart cannot pump blood past the high pressure in the lungs during exercise. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would carry an unacceptably high risk of fatal exertional syncope or irreversible right ventricular ischemia. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm the right heart strain, reinstitute potent diuretic therapy, and mandate strict bed rest pending urgent echocardiography and specialist cardiology review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered an immediate dose of oral Furosemide 40mg to initiate gentle fluid offloading. Advised strict rest and avoidance of any exertion. Generated an urgent referral for a comprehensive echocardiogram and Respiratory/Cardiology review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Weigh yourself every single morning using the exact same scales before you eat breakfast; writing this specific number on the calendar provides undeniable proof of dangerous fluid building up around your heart, allowing us to adjust your water tablets before you end up in the emergency room.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair mandated to prevent presyncope during bathing.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow to assess fluid status; patient must attend the urgent echocardiogram (to estimate mPAP) within 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation (call 000) if the patient actually loses consciousness (faints), if they develop severe central chest pain, or if breathlessness occurs while sitting completely still.

